# Bloodline Accessibility Rules

## Core Accessibility Philosophy

Accessibility is mandatory.

The platform must remain:
- readable
- navigable
- semantic
- keyboard-accessible
- motion-safe

Premium presentation must never reduce usability.

---

## Semantic HTML Rules

Use semantic HTML whenever possible.

Preferred:

```tsx
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<article>
<footer>
<button>
```

Avoid:
- div-heavy layout abuse
- non-semantic clickable wrappers
- meaningless container nesting

---

## Heading Hierarchy Rules

Maintain proper hierarchy.

Required:
- one H1 per page
- logical H2 progression
- logical H3 structure

Avoid:
- skipped heading levels
- decorative heading misuse
- multiple H1s

Headings should support:
- SEO
- screen readers
- readability

---

## Button Rules

Interactive actions must use:
```tsx
<button>
```

Navigation actions must use:
```tsx
<a>
```

Avoid:
- clickable divs
- non-semantic interaction wrappers

---

## Link Rules

Links should:
- have meaningful labels
- remain visually identifiable
- maintain accessible focus states

Avoid:
- vague "click here" links
- hidden focus behavior
- inaccessible icon-only links without labels

---

## Keyboard Navigation Rules

All interactive systems must support:
- keyboard navigation
- logical tab order
- visible focus states

Required for:
- menus
- modals
- dialogs
- filters
- carousels
- CTA systems

Avoid:
- keyboard traps
- inaccessible overlays
- hidden focus states

---

## Focus State Rules

Focus states must remain:
- visible
- intentional
- accessible

Avoid:
- removing focus outlines globally
- invisible focus indicators

Focus styling should feel:
- premium
- tactical
- readable

---

## Contrast Rules

Maintain readable contrast across:
- typography
- buttons
- overlays
- cards
- accent elements

Avoid:
- low-contrast muted text
- unreadable overlay combinations
- aesthetic-first contrast failures

Luxury styling must remain readable.

---

## Typography Accessibility Rules

Typography should remain:
- readable
- scalable
- responsive

Avoid:
- ultra-thin text
- tiny paragraph sizing
- unreadable condensed body text

Uppercase headings are allowed,
but body copy should remain highly readable.

---

## Motion Accessibility Rules

Always support:
```txt
prefers-reduced-motion
```

Reduce:
- reveal animations
- hover motion
- scrolling motion

when reduced motion is requested.

Avoid:
- forcing motion-heavy experiences

---

## Image Accessibility Rules

All meaningful images require:
- descriptive alt text

Decorative images should use:
```tsx
alt=""
```

Avoid:
- keyword-stuffed alt text
- meaningless alt labels
- missing alt attributes

---

## Gallery Accessibility Rules

Gallery systems should support:
- keyboard navigation
- accessible modals
- readable labels
- logical focus flow

Avoid:
- inaccessible lightboxes
- keyboard-inaccessible carousels

---

## Form Accessibility Rules

All forms must support:
- labels
- focus states
- accessible validation
- keyboard usability

Avoid:
- placeholder-only labels
- inaccessible validation messaging

---

## Modal Accessibility Rules

Modals must:
- trap focus correctly
- restore focus on close
- support keyboard close
- support escape key

Avoid:
- inaccessible overlay systems
- hidden keyboard behavior

---

## Mobile Accessibility Rules

Mobile UI should support:
- readable tap targets
- readable spacing
- accessible typography
- stable interaction zones

Avoid:
- tiny buttons
- cramped interactions
- inaccessible sticky elements

---

## Screen Reader Rules

Use:
- semantic structure
- aria labels where needed
- meaningful button labels

Avoid:
- excessive aria usage
- redundant labels
- hidden inaccessible structures

---

## Aria Rules

Use aria only when necessary.

Preferred:
- semantic HTML first
- aria enhancement second

Avoid:
- unnecessary aria spam
- incorrect aria usage

---

## Error State Rules

Error states should:
- remain readable
- explain issues clearly
- preserve accessibility

Avoid:
- color-only error communication
- hidden validation failures

---

## Loading State Rules

Loading states should:
- remain accessible
- avoid layout shifting
- communicate state clearly

Avoid:
- inaccessible animated loaders
- hidden async state changes

---

## Responsive Accessibility Rules

Accessibility must remain consistent across:
- desktop
- tablet
- mobile

Avoid:
- mobile-only accessibility regressions
- hidden mobile navigation traps

---

## SEO Accessibility Rules

Accessibility supports SEO.

Maintain:
- semantic structure
- heading hierarchy
- crawlable content
- meaningful labels

Avoid:
- accessibility-breaking SEO hacks

---

## AI Accessibility Safety Rules

AI-generated UI must:
- remain semantic
- preserve keyboard navigation
- preserve focus visibility
- maintain readable hierarchy

Avoid:
- div-only interaction systems
- inaccessible custom controls
- visual-only UX decisions

---

## Forbidden Patterns

Never introduce:

- clickable divs
- hidden focus states
- inaccessible modals
- keyboard traps
- low-contrast typography
- missing alt text
- multiple H1s
- placeholder-only forms
- inaccessible hover-only interactions

---

## Final Principle

Every accessibility decision should reinforce:

- usability
- readability
- trust
- professionalism
- inclusivity
- SEO quality
- premium UX

If a visual or interaction decision weakens accessibility, do not implement it.